After the course, a student should be able to:

show knowledge of basic genetics, and understanding of how inheritance is governed by molecular mechanisms;
explain how inheritance is studied at the level of cells and organisms, and how allele frequencies in populations vary with different factors;
test, interpret and statistically evaluate results;
study, present and discuss original literature in English;
reflect on ethical problems in the application of genetic knowledge.

Running through every part, a student should be able to:

B1. find a gene, variant, disease or organism in the core public databases (NCBI, Ensembl, OMIM, ClinVar, gnomAD, model-organism databases) and extract a specific fact;
B2. run a sequence similarity search (BLAST) and read the result;
B3. search PubMed for original papers on a genetic question;
B4. use R to test genetic ratios and Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium (χ²);
B5. use R to simulate simple genetic processes, such as crosses, drift and bottlenecks;
B6. load a downloaded table or sequence (TSV, CSV, FASTA) into R and summarise or plot it;
B7. write small R functions of their own.

Step 4: the two distances
Each interval’s recombination frequency counts every offspring with a crossover in that interval, so the double crossovers count in both:
RF(cv\text{–}ct) = \frac{\text{SCO in I} + \text{DCO}}{N} = \frac{129 + 11}{2000} = 0.070 \;\Rightarrow\; \mathbf{7.0\ cM}
RF(ct\text{–}v) = \frac{\text{SCO in II} + \text{DCO}}{N} = \frac{249 + 11}{2000} = 0.130 \;\Rightarrow\; \mathbf{13.0\ cM}
cv\text{–}v = 7.0 + 13.0 = \mathbf{20.0\ cM}

The common mistake is to leave the DCOs out of the interval distances. A double crossover chromatid has a crossover in region I and one in region II, so it is a recombinant for both intervals. The outer distance is the sum of the two intervals. In map units from the previous unit, 1 percent recombination is 1 centimorgan; for short intervals like these, RF and map distance are nearly the same, so we do not need a map function here.

Coefficient of coincidence and interference

\text{CoC} = \frac{\text{observed DCO}}{\text{expected DCO}} \qquad I = 1 - \text{CoC}

The coefficient of coincidence is a ratio, observed over expected double crossovers. Interference is its complement. Read the scale: CoC 1, I 0, means crossovers in the two intervals are independent. CoC 0, I 1, means complete interference: no double crossovers at all, one crossover blocks the other. Values in between, as here, mean partial interference. A CoC above 1, negative interference, means more doubles than expected; that is rare in standard crosses and is seen, for example, over very short distances in some fungi and bacteriophage crosses, where it reflects a different mechanism. In this course, expect positive interference.

By the end of this exercise you should be able to:

store the 8 classes of a three-point cross as a tibble and pick out the parental and double crossover classes with slice_max() and slice_min();
write a function that, for a given gene order, returns the distances, the expected DCOs, the CoC and the interference as a one-row tibble;
run that function over all three possible orders with map(), stack the results with list_rbind(), and pick the order where the double crossovers are rarest;
simulate three-point crosses to see how noisy the coefficient of coincidence is.

