Find it
ClinVar collects reports of human variants and what they do to health. Each record has a Variation ID, an HGVS name, the molecular consequence (missense, nonsense, …), a germline classification (pathogenic, benign, …), a review status (how well supported the classification is) and links, including the variant’s dbSNP rs number. dbSNP gives each variable position an rs number and lists its alleles on the + strand of the reference genome. You met HbS and HbC in Genetics as information.
F1. (HbS) Open the ClinVar record VCV000015333 (or search ClinVar for HBB[gene] AND c.20A&gt;T). Read:

the HGVS name in the title, and the protein change;
the molecular consequence;
the germline classification and the review status;
the dbSNP ID, and the location on GRCh38.

Is c.20A&gt;T a transition or a transversion?
F2. (HbC) Open VCV000015126. Read the HGVS name, the molecular consequence and the dbSNP ID. Which transcript version does the title use, and why does that not matter for this variant? Is c.19G&gt;A a transition or a transversion? Compare the genomic position with HbS.
F3. (a nonsense variant) Search ClinVar for CFTR[gene] AND G542X and open the record whose title ends in (p.Gly542Ter). Report:

the HGVS name and the Variation ID;
the molecular consequence and the condition;
the classification and the review status;
the dbSNP ID. Why is the legacy name “G542X”?

F4. (one variant, two strands) Open rs334 in dbSNP. Read the Alleles field and the position on GRCh38 (NC_000011.10).

Why does dbSNP write T&gt;A, while ClinVar writes c.20A&gt;T for the same HbS change?
dbSNP lists three possible alternative alleles at this position. Which one is HbS?







